.\" Manual page for the compare command
.TH COMPARE 1 "October 2026" "compare 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
compare \- longest common subsequence of two files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B compare
[\fIoptions\fR]
.I file1 file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B compare
computes the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) of
.I file1
and
.IR file2 .
By default it prints their differences as an edit script; with
.B -C
it prints the common (matching) records instead, and with
.B -l
it prints only the LCS length.
.PP
Each difference is introduced by a header line:
.RS
.nf
\fB<<<<<\fR n \fIfile1\fR [\fIbegin\fR:\fIend\fR] \fB<<<<<\fR
        (records present only in \fIfile1\fR)
\fB>>>>>\fR n \fIfile2\fR [\fIbegin\fR:\fIend\fR] \fB>>>>>\fR
        (records present only in \fIfile2\fR)
.fi
.RE
where
.I n
is the one-based difference number and
.I [begin:end]
the half-open range of records involved.  A final line reports the LCS
length, and when compiled with duration reporting enabled, a timing line
such as
\fIRickSpace: 28 operations in 9 \[u00B5]s @3.111 MHz\fR
follows.
.PP
The two files are compared at one of three granularities:
.TP
.B record
each input line is one record (the default);
.TP
.B text
.RB ( -T )
the whole file is one sequence of Unicode code points, so multi-byte
characters are never split;
.TP
.B word
.RB ( -w )
each whitespace-separated word is one record.
.PP
Four LCS algorithms — or automatic dispatch — are available through
.BR -a :
.TP
.B a
Automatic (the default): heuristic dispatch that selects the algorithm from
the input's edit distance, match count, and alphabet size.
.TP
.B d
Myers (1986): the O(ND) minimal edit-script algorithm
.RB ( N
the sum of the two input lengths,
.I D
the edit distance); fastest on near-identical input.
.TP
.B m
Hunt-Szymanski (1977): a threshold array over the match
lists, O((r+n) log n) time.
.TP
.B s
RickSpace (Rick 2000): forward/backward contours plus Hirschberg recursion,
O(ns) space.
.TP
.B t
RickTime (Rick 1995): a single diagonal sweep, O(ns + d) space.
.PP
Automatic dispatch selects Myers when the edit distance is small,
Hunt-Szymanski when the matches are sparse, RickTime when its match
recording fits in memory, and RickSpace otherwise.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -a " a|d|m|s|t"
Select the LCS algorithm:
.B a
(Automatic, the default),
.B d
(Myers),
.B m
(Hunt-Szymanski),
.B s
(RickSpace), or
.B t
(RickTime).  Accepts
.B -ad
or
.BR "-a d" .
.TP
.B -b
Ignore whitespace: collapse each run of whitespace to a single space before
comparing, and expand the reported spans back to the original positions.
.TP
.B -C
Print the correspondence (the common, matching records) instead of the
differences.
.TP
.B -H
Print host information (name, locale, processor, and memory) and exit.
.TP
.B -F
Treat the input files as FASTA and strip header lines; implies
.BR -T .
.TP
.B -h
Print a usage synopsis and exit.
.TP
.B -i
Perform a case-insensitive comparison.
.TP
.BI -j " join"
Coalesce nearby differences that lie within
.I join
records of each other.  In unified
.B -u
mode it also merges intervals within that distance.
.TP
.B -l
Report only the LCS length; skip reconstructing the differences.
.TP
.BI -p " prefix"
Print
.I prefix
lines of context before each difference.
.TP
.B -Q
Treat
.I file1
and
.I file2
as literal strings rather than filenames; implies
.BR -T .
.TP
.BI -s " suffix"
Print
.I suffix
lines of context after each difference.
.TP
.BI -t " tabsize"
Set the tab size used to expand tab characters in the parallel
.BR -y " p"
output (default 8).
.TP
.B -T
Compare the whole file as a sequence of Unicode code points (the text path)
rather than line-by-line records.
.TP
.B -u
Emit a GNU unified diff patch of the two files (the Edit format) instead of
the default differences view.  Context lines come from
.B -p
(prefix) and
.B -s
(suffix), defaulting to 3 for whichever is not given explicitly.
.B -u
is incompatible with
.BR -C ,
.BR -F ,
.BR -l ,
.BR -Q ,
.BR -T ,
and
.BR -y .
.BR -w
with
.B -u
is not yet implemented.
.TP
.B -v
Enable verbose output: print the LCS length, the start and finish wall-clock
times, and a timing summary; by default only the differences are shown.
.TP
.B -V
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B -w
Split each line into whitespace-separated words and compare word by word
(record mode).
.TP
.BI -W " width"
Set the per-side content width used by the parallel
.BR -y " p"
format (default 80, minimum 5).  Content is truncated to this width, so the
total output line is 1 + 2 x width characters.
.TP
.BI -y " c|p|s"
Select the output format:
.B c
(CIGAR alignment string; file1 is the reference and file2 the query),
.B p
(parallel: side-by-side columns), or
.B s
(sequential, the default: one side after the other).
.PP
The
.BR -h ,
.BR -H ,
and
.B -V
switches are early-exit switches: each prints its report and terminates
without comparing files.
.SH ALGORITHM
The
.B -a
selector chooses among four algorithms, each fastest in its own regime.
Three independent properties of the input decide which regime applies:
.TP
Edit distance
.IR D :
insertions plus deletions of the minimal edit script.
.TP
Redundancy \[*r]
.IR r/n :
average matches per position; low for sparse matches, high for skewed or
small alphabets.
.TP
Alphabet size
.IR s :
number of distinct symbols, which sizes the Rick table builds.
.PP
The algorithms and their regimes:
.TP
.B d
Myers (edit distance): fastest on near-identical input (small
.IR D ).
The O(ND) minimal edit-script algorithm
.RB ( N
the sum of the two input lengths); its reconstruction history grows
with the square of
.IR D ,
so it is bounded by a memory-derived budget.
.TP
.B m
Hunt-Szymanski (matches): fastest when the matches are sparse (low
\[*r], large alphabet), the regime its threshold array is built for.
.TP
.B s
RickSpace (space): the linear-memory fallback; O(ns) space via Hirschberg
recursion, so it never exhausts memory and completes when no other
algorithm does.
.TP
.B t
RickTime (time): fastest at high redundancy (high \[*r]), when its
dominant-match recording fits in memory.
.PP
Automatic dispatch (
.BR -aa ,
the default) estimates
.I D
and
.I r
from the symbol counts and selects in order:
.RS
.IP 1. 4
Myers when the edit distance lower bound is at most 2000;
.IP 2. 4
Hunt-Szymanski when the match count is at most 100 times the shorter
input length (sparse matches);
.IP 3. 4
RickTime when its recording budget covers the shorter input;
.IP 4. 4
RickSpace otherwise.
.RE
.PP
.B -v
reports which algorithm automatic dispatch selected and why.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including
.BR -h ,
.BR -V ,
and
.BR -H ).
.TP
.B 1
Error: a command-line parse failure, a missing file, or an algorithm failure.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The locale (in particular
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_NUMERIC )
controls the thousands separator used in the reported counts and the locale
name shown by
.BR -H .
On a system whose locale defines digit grouping, counts are printed with
comma separators (for example
.BR "LCS Length = 4,591,960" );
otherwise they are printed ungrouped.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Compare two files line by line and show the differences:
.nf
.RS
compare old.txt new.txt
.RE
.fi
.TP
Compare side by side (parallel output) with 40-character sides and 2-space tab stops:
.nf
.RS
compare -yp -W 40 -t 2 old.txt new.txt
.RE
.fi
.TP
Compare as text (code points), case-insensitively, showing only the length:
.nf
.RS
compare -i -l -T a.fa b.fa
.RE
.fi
.TP
Compare two FASTA genomes with the linear-space algorithm and show matches:
.nf
.RS
compare -as -C -F ecoli.seq ecoli_100subs.seq
.RE
.fi
.TP
Compare two literal strings rather than files:
.nf
.RS
compare -Q "the quick sly fox jumped" "over the lazy brown dog"
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR diff (1),
.BR cmp (1)
.PP
The LCS algorithms and their performance bounds are described in
.I LCSRickSpace.md
and
.I LCSRickTime.md
in the project documentation.
